,{"date":"2026-10-22","committee":"Adur Council","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-05","committee":"Joint Overview & Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-07-07","committee":"Joint Audit and Governance Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-09","committee":"Joint Overview & Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-16","committee":"Adur Council","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-20","committee":"Joint Audit & Governance Sub-Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-21","committee":"Worthing Council","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Worthing's treasury budget significantly underspent partly because project interest was capitalized to avoid revenue overspend on Integrated Care Centre","lead":"Interest capitalization deferred revenue pressure; without it, the council would have faced a \u00a3300k overspend on treasury costs, indicating tight affordability margins on this major project","iso":"2026-07-07","paper_title":"Joint Annual Treasury Management Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/documents/s17216/17216.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Energy price shock threatens further inflation spike and rate increases: treasury advisors flag risk of CPI breaching 4.5% later in 2026","lead":"If energy-driven inflation spike materializes, refinancing costs will be significantly higher than currently budgeted; lenders assess councils' resilience to sustained high rate environment","iso":"2026-07-07","paper_title":"Joint Annual Treasury Management Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/documents/s17216/17216.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Interest rate environment sharply reversed from expectation: Bank Rate held at 3.75% and treasury advisors now expect rises to 4-4.25%, not cuts","lead":"Refinancing of maturing debt (23-25% of portfolio under 12 months) will occur at materially higher rates than forecast; both councils face higher financing costs and must refinance debt when rates are elevated","iso":"2026-07-07","paper_title":"Joint Annual Treasury Management Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/documents/s17216/17216.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-07-07","committee":"Joint Audit and Governance Committee","paper_title":"Joint Annual Treasury Management Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/documents/s17216/17216.pdf","headline":"Worthing's treasury budget significantly underspent partly because project interest was capitalized to avoid revenue overspend on Integrated Care Centre","detail":"Worthing's treasury management budgets were 'significantly underspent' in 2025-26, largely due to capitalization of interest on the Worthing Integrated Care Centre Project. Paper notes 'had this interest not been charged there would have been an estimated \u00a3300k overspend in treasury management.' This accounting choice avoids revenue impact on a major capital project but masks underlying financing pressure.","category":"capital","matters":"Interest capitalization deferred revenue pressure; without it, the council would have faced a \u00a3300k overspend on treasury costs, indicating tight affordability margins on this major project","figures":"\u00a3300k potential revenue overspend if interest not capitalized; actual treasury underspend due to capitalization","quote":"Overall the treasury management budgets for Worthing were significantly underspent for the financial year 2025/26. This was largely as a result of the capitalisation of interest chargeable on the Worthing Integrated Care Centre Project. Had this interest not been charged there would have been an estimated \u00a3300k overspend in treasury management.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-07","committee":"Joint Audit and Governance Committee","paper_title":"Joint Annual Treasury Management Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/documents/s17216/17216.pdf","headline":"Energy price shock threatens further inflation spike and rate increases: treasury advisors flag risk of CPI breaching 4.5% later in 2026","detail":"Economic summary notes 'recent upward pressure on energy costs could see CPI inflation breach 4.5% later this year' and warns 'the Middle East conflict is likely to see inflation spike higher from late spring 2026 through to early 2027.' This directly counters the previous expectation of rate cuts. Higher inflation would force Bank of England to hold or raise rates, increasing borrowing costs. Public finances are already under pressure with government debt interest at highest levels since June 2025.","category":"other","matters":"If energy-driven inflation spike materializes, refinancing costs will be significantly higher than currently budgeted; lenders assess councils' resilience to sustained high rate environment","figures":"CPI February 3.0% (peaked July-Sept at 3.8%), forecast potential breach of 4.5%, government debt interest payment highest since June 2025 at \u00a313.0bn","quote":"inflation concerns remained elevated in March 2026...the recent upward pressure on energy costs could see CPI inflation breach 4.5% later this year...the Middle East conflict is likely to see inflation spike higher from late spring 2026 through to early 2027","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-07","committee":"Joint Audit and Governance Committee","paper_title":"Joint Annual Treasury Management Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/documents/s17216/17216.pdf","headline":"Interest rate environment sharply reversed from expectation: Bank Rate held at 3.75% and treasury advisors now expect rises to 4-4.25%, not cuts","detail":"Initial interest rate forecasts in early 2025 expected 'gradual reductions in short, medium and longer-term fixed borrowing rates.' Actual outturn: Bank Rate reduced only to 3.75% and inflation concerns prevented further cuts. By 31 March 2026, 'the market expected Bank Rate to increase over the coming months to 4% or 4.25%' with 'all parts of the curve risen substantially through March.' PWLB rates: 1-year 5.04%, 5-year 5.28%, 10-year 5.72%.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Refinancing of maturing debt (23-25% of portfolio under 12 months) will occur at materially higher rates than forecast; both councils face higher financing costs and must refinance debt when rates are elevated","figures":"Bank Rate 3.75%; 1-year PWLB 5.04%; 5-year PWLB 5.28%; 10-year PWLB 5.72%; 23-25% debt matures under 12 months","quote":"Interest rate forecasts initially suggested gradual reductions in short, medium and longer-term fixed borrowing rates during 2025/26. Bank Rate did reduce to 3.75% as anticipated, but the initial expectation of significant rate reductions did not transpire, primarily because inflation concerns remained elevated in March 2026. At the start of April 2026, the market expected Bank Rate to increase over the coming months to 4% or 4.25%","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-07","committee":"Joint Audit and Governance Committee","paper_title":"Joint Annual Treasury Management Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/documents/s17216/17216.pdf","headline":"Worthing's two \u00a35m housing loans to Worthing Homes mature in 2027 and 2028, ending \u00a370k annual net revenue contribution","detail":"Worthing made two loans of \u00a35m each for 10 years to Worthing Homes to support housing delivery. The council receives \u00a370k per annum net (above the cost of PWLB borrowing). These loans mature in 2027 and 2028. This income stream is temporary and will end within 12-24 months, potentially impacting treasury returns and requiring replacement investment strategy.","category":"income","matters":"Loss of \u00a370k annual net revenue income from maturing loans reduces recurring income available for council services; lenders may scrutinize replacement capital spending plans if housing programme cannot absorb the loss","figures":"Two \u00a35m loans, total \u00a310m, \u00a370k annual net return, maturity 2027 and 2028","quote":"Worthing Borough Council has made two loans of \u00a35m each for 10 years to Worthing Homes to support   the building of homes.   The Council receives \u00a370k per annum net in interest over and above the cost to the Council of borrowing the \u00a310m from the Public Works Loan Board...The loans are fully secured on property and mature in 2027 and 2028.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-07","committee":"Joint Audit and Governance Committee","paper_title":"Joint Annual Treasury Management Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/documents/s17216/17216.pdf","headline":"Chichester College restructured debt during 2025-26, requiring Worthing to obtain independent legal advice on its \u00a35m loan security","detail":"Worthing made a \u00a35m loan to GB Met College (now Chichester College Group) in January 2020 for 20 years to support local education. During 2025-26 the college restructured its wider debt, triggering Worthing to seek external legal advice (costs borne by the college). Advice concluded 'no material changes' but the college's new financing is 'in part secured on the same site' as Worthing's loan, with Worthing's security ranking 'ahead of other creditors'. The fact that restructuring occurred and legal review was necessary signals counterparty financial stress.","category":"other","matters":"Counterparty financial weakness (requiring debt restructuring) on a \u00a35m 20-year loan creates repayment risk even with first security ranking; lenders may be concerned about covenant compliance and the college's operational sustainability","figures":"\u00a35m loan advanced January 2020, 20-year term, security ranking: first creditor position","quote":"During the 2025/26 year Chichester College Group restructured other elements of its debt, impacting on some elements of the original agreement. Officers sought advice...which concluded that there were no material changes to the terms of our loan agreement. The new loan financing agreed by the college, is in part secured on the same site as the loan made by this council, however WBC's security continues to rank ahead of other creditors","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-07-07"},"name":"Adur","findings_total":202,"news_drafts_total":3}}